// @METADATA:name        = paper_p5
// @METADATA:description = Program P5 from Figure 8 of the paper.
//                         Illustrates observe-nontermination dependence
//                         inside a conditional branch.
//                         When y>=3: observe(y=4) conditions on y.
//                         When y<3:  while(true) diverges — nonterminating
//                         loop on the else branch.
//                         The if-predicate (line 3) controls whether we
//                         observe or diverge. Under ns, the if-predicate
//                         is scd-dependent on return x (nonterminating
//                         path never reaches return). Under nids/ni,
//                         the if-predicate may not be wcd-dependent on
//                         return x since return post-dominates if under
//                         the termination assumption.
// @METADATA:reference   = Masud and Olmedo (2026) Static Nontermination-
//                         (In)Sensitive Slicing of Probabilistic Programs.
//                         Figure 8, Program P5.
// @METADATA:criterion   = x
// @METADATA:expected    = ns: entire program in slice (if-predicate scd
//                         on return; observe and while both kept);
//                         nids: observe kept; while(true) and if may be
//                         sliced since y not in data dep of x;
//                         ni: most aggressive; observe may be sliced if
//                         y independent of x
// @METADATA:tags        = observe, nontermination, conditional, obsntd,
//                         paper-example, figure-8, ns-vs-nids-vs-ni,
//                         key-example

x :~ unif[1, 4];
y :~ unif[1, 4];
if y >= 3 then
    observe(y = 4);
else
    while true do
        skip;
    end
end
return x;